The graft command-line client.

Usage: graft [OPTIONS] <COMMAND>

Commands:
  login      Store credentials for a named server, or list configured servers
  logout     Remove a named server and its stored token
  config     Inspect user configuration
  clone      Open a workspace and lay out a sparse working copy
  status     Working-copy changes against the head draft, else the base (no server needed)
  diff       Diff the working copy, or two changesets
  log        First-parent history
  show       A changeset with its patch
  blame      Line origins for a file
  cat        Print a file at a changeset
  ls         List a directory at a changeset
  sparse     List, add or remove the slice's path prefixes
  commit     Upload the slice's changes and create a draft on the server
  workspace  Show, list, open, renew or close workspaces
  line       Create or list lines
  tag        Create or list immutable tags
  stack      List, squash, reorder, split or drop the drafts between the base and the head
  sync       Move the base to the line tip (the server rebases a stack; conflicts are written into the files)
  land       Land the stack on the line
  revert     Add a draft that inverts a changeset
  pick       Add a draft that re-applies a landed changeset (a cherry-pick; conflicts are written into the files)
  api        Call a read operation of the API, with JSON in and out
  version    Print versions
  mcp        Run the MCP server over stdio, for agents
  lsp        Run the LSP server over stdio
  help       Print this message or the help of the given subcommand(s)

Options:
      --json               Print stable JSON; its schema is published with the release
      --endpoint <URL>     Where to send requests: `http://host:port`, `https://…`, or `unix:/path`
      --repo <NAME>        Repository name (default: the working copy's)
      --token-file <PATH>  File holding the bearer token. `GRAFT_TOKEN` is the other source. A token is never taken from an argument
  -C, --directory <DIR>    Run as if started in this directory, like `git -C`
  -h, --help               Print help

Server roles:
  serve   Run the authoritative server
  node    Run the host cache daemon
  mirror  Import, follow, batch or export git repositories
